Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Great upper body gains this week, but your legs urgently need more attention.",
  "summary": "This was a solid week for upper body development. Chest and back both showed meaningful strength gains, and you moved a healthy total tonnage of 8,732 kg across 23 sets. Your consistency on the big upper body movers is paying off and aligns well with your muscle-building goal. That said, legs received only 1 set this entire period, which is a significant gap that needs to be addressed right away to build a balanced, strong physique.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Progressive overload is clearly happening in the major muscle groups. Chest climbed from an index of 104 to 108, and back followed the same pattern from 104 to 107 — both are meaningful jumps in a single period. Shoulders ticked up slightly from 102 to 103, and triceps moved from 103 to 104, both heading in the right direction. Biceps held flat at 101, which is worth monitoring. Legs also held flat at 100 with only 1 set logged, so there is essentially no stimulus being applied there to drive adaptation. Overall the overload trend is positive for the upper body, but legs are not progressing at all."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "severe",
      "observation": "Legs received just 1 set and account for only 4.6% of total volume this period. For a muscle-building goal, legs are drastically undertrained relative to every other muscle group and relative to any reasonable programming standard for a 28-year-old male."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps received 3 sets at 4.9% of volume and showed no strength index change this period. While the absolute volume is low, the flat index suggests the current stimulus may not be enough to drive growth alongside the stronger progress seen in chest and back."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Dramatically increase leg training next period — aim for at least 4 to 6 sets targeting quads, hamstrings, and glutes. Squats, Romanian deadlifts, or leg press are all excellent choices that will drive the tonnage and strength index upward.",
    "Add 1 to 2 additional sets for biceps next period and consider increasing the load or varying the exercise to break through the current plateau at an index of 101.",
    "Maintain the current chest and back approach — the strength gains are excellent, so keep the progressive overload going by adding small amounts of weight or an extra rep where possible.",
    "Keep a close eye on overall recovery as total volume grows, especially once legs are added back in more seriously. The previous period's note flagged recovery as something to watch, and adding leg volume will meaningfully increase total systemic fatigue."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strong upper body progress with chest and back strength indices both rising to 107–108, but legs were severely undertrained at just 1 set; the priority next period is restoring leg volume to balance the program."
}
